<div class="bb-generic-hosts-instructions" id="bb-generic-hosts-instructions-generic">
<h3>Steps (Proxmox VE / Debian / Ubuntu)</h3>
<ol>
    <li>Open a root shell on the remote host (Proxmox: the node's console or SSH as root).</li>
    <li>Paste and run the setup command above.</li>
    <li>Follow its output. It creates the user with a locked password, applies the ZFS delegations,
    writes the forced-command SSH key line and adds an SSH restriction drop-in.</li>
    <li>Re-run with <code>--verify</code> to confirm everything is in place.</li>
</ol>
</div>

<div class="bb-generic-hosts-instructions" id="bb-generic-hosts-instructions-scale" style="display:none;">
<h3>Steps (TrueNAS SCALE)</h3>
<ol>
    <li><b>SSH service</b>: System Settings → Services → SSH: enable it and set the TCP port (matches the port above).</li>
    <li><b>User</b>: Credentials → Users → Add: use the username above, disable password login, and pick a normal shell (e.g. /usr/bin/bash).</li>
    <li><b>SSH key</b>: paste the public key above into the user's "SSH Public Key" field in the UI.</li>
    <li><b>Run the setup script</b> from a root shell (TrueNAS: <code>sudo -i</code> in the web shell). It installs the
    command allowlist, applies the ZFS delegations and rewrites the user's authorized_keys with the forced-command line.</li>
    <li>If the script says the zfs binary is not executable for your user, paste the values it prints into the user's
    <b>"Allowed sudo commands with no password"</b> field in the UI and re-run the script.</li>
    <li><b>SSH restriction</b>: paste the <code>Match</code> block printed by the script into the SSH service's
    <b>"Auxiliary Parameters"</b> field (System Settings → Services → SSH).</li>
    <li><b>Important</b>: editing the user's keys in the TrueNAS UI later rewrites authorized_keys and removes the
    forced-command line. Re-run the setup script (it is idempotent) afterwards.</li>
</ol>
</div>

<h3>Security model</h3>
<p>
The remote host only ever gets the minimum access it needs, never root, and never a shell:
<ul>
    <li><b>Receiver</b> (this Unraid pushes): the user may create and receive datasets under the configured parent
    (and serve restores back), but cannot delete, list other pools' data, or run anything outside the allowlist.
    Received data is never mounted on the remote host (<code>mountpoint=none</code>).</li>
    <li><b>Sender</b> (this Unraid pulls): the user may send snapshots of the configured dataset and nothing more.
    If the remote host runs OpenZFS 2.4+, sends are restricted to raw streams, so this Unraid cannot read the
    remote data even if this server is compromised.</li>
    <li>SSH access is command-restricted (forced command), non-interactive, with no port forwarding and no pty.</li>
    <li>Backups are always raw encrypted snapshot streams: the remote end cannot read your data.</li>
</ul>
Raw encrypted sends require <code>feature@encryption</code> on the destination pool. The "Test connection"
button on a backup entry probes what it can and tells you what to verify manually.
</p>
</div>
